     1	# Constrained shuffle into `(ab)*` — arXiv:1707.04310
     2	
     3	Status: all local gates pass, but the required four-vendor oracle verdict is
     4	**not yet available**. The script-owned run on 2026-09-05 received HTTP 403
     5	“Key limit exceeded” on every redraw. Those calls are errors, not model
     6	failures, so this directory must not be submitted until `harden.py` completes.
     7	
     8	| Profile field | Value |
     9	|---|---|
    10	| Track | B — no-tool compression |
    11	| Native domain | combinatorics |
    12	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    13	| Computational core | permutation |
    14	| Certificate | integer-tuple set-system partition |
    15	| Intended intuition | invariant: project onto power-of-two positions |
    16	| Domain essentiality | native |
    17	| Reduction | none |
    18	
    19	## The family
    20	
    21	The source is Amarilli and Paperman, [*Topological Sorting under Regular
    22	Constraints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04310). Section 2 defines constrained
    23	shuffle: the solver is handed a tuple of words, equivalently a disjoint union
    24	of labeled directed paths, and must interleave them while preserving each
    25	path’s internal order. Here the fixed target language is `(ab)*`.
    26	
    27	Every instance contains pointwise-complementary pairs of equal-length path
    28	words. The requested witness is a partition of the path indices into those
    29	pairs. For each pair, reading its `a` at a column and then its `b` produces
    30	`ab`; concatenating the pairs gives a legal topological order. `verify` checks
    31	the index partition and every symbol comparison exactly. It never reads the
    32	planted answer.
    33	
    34	## Why Track B, and what is actually hard
    35	
    36	Theorem 3.6 proves that unrestricted `CSh[(ab)*]` is NP-hard, using the Section
    37	3 shuffle reduction from the unary 3-PARTITION family in Lemma 3.2. That is not
    38	a distributional hardness claim for this generator. This subclass has a known
    39	linear algorithm: hash all words and look up each pointwise complement. Its
    40	complexity is `O(nL)` time and storage; at the shipping preset the measured
    41	mean is below 0.001 seconds and the accounting is 43,029 symbol/hash operations.
    42	
    43	The no-tool compression is a projection. At 1-based positions 1, 2, 4, 8, 16,
    44	and 32, every long word carries a unique six-symbol signature whose complement
    45	identifies its partner. Reading 42 signatures and doing 21 lookups costs 273
    46	operations. A solver that misses this invariant must process 21,504 displayed
    47	symbols or reproduce the reference hashing route unaided.
    48	
    49	The easy regimes were checked explicitly. Theorem 4.3 puts monomial target
    50	languages in NL. Proposition C.2 gives an `O(k log n)`-space nondeterministic
    51	algorithm for width `k`, and for CSh takes `k` to be the number of input
    52	strings; it also notes a prior PTIME result. Neither is hidden here as Track-A
    53	evidence. The successful special-purpose complement algorithm is reported as
    54	the Track-B reference algorithm.
    55	
    56	## Worked demo
    57	
    58	For `make_instance(n=4, word_length=8, signature_bits=2, seed=0)`, the complete
    59	word table is:
    60	
    61	```text
    62	0: aabbaabb
    63	1: baabaabb
    64	2: abbabbaa
    65	3: bbaabbaa
    66	```
    67	
    68	The answer is `[[0,3],[1,2]]`. Calling `verify(inst, [[0,3],[1,2]])` returns
    69	`(True, "ok")`. The corrupted partition `[[0,1],[2,3]]` returns
    70	`(False, "paths 0 and 1 are not pointwise complements")`. A person can solve
    71	this demo on paper by comparing the four eight-symbol rows.
    72	
    73	## Difficulty presets
    74	
    75	| Preset | Paths `n` | Word length `L` | Signature bits | Answer atoms | Status |
    76	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    77	| demo | 4 | 8 | 2 | 4 | hand-scale illustration |
    78	| easy | 22 | 64 | 5 | 22 | oracle run attempted, API error |
    79	| medium | 34 | 256 | 6 | 34 | not reached |
    80	| hard | 42 | 512 | 6 | 42 | intended shipping preset |
    81	
    82	`escalate` first doubles word length while leaving the 42-index witness fixed;
    83	only after that axis is exhausted does it increase the number of paths.
    84	
    85	## Gate results
    86	
    87	| Gate | Result |
    88	|---|---|
    89	| G1 planted verifies | 12/12 |
    90	| G2 corruption | 5/5 rejected with five distinct reasons |
    91	| G3 round trip | passed with prose and fenced JSON |
    92	| G4 structure-aware guessing | 0/200,000; matching space 13,113,070,457,687,988,603,440,625 |
    93	| G5 density and baseline | 0/200,000 shipping guesses; demo has exactly 1 answer; 43,029 reference operations |
    94	| G6 attacks | four attacks, each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
    95	| G7 scaling | 84 paths of length 1,024 builds and verifies |
    96	| G8 canonical key | 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
    97	| G9(c) caps | 200 chars, 127 conservative tokens, 42 atoms, 273 operations |
    98	
    99	## Oracle loop and G9 arms
   100	
   101	The bare run stopped before a scored attempt. `harden.py` made four redraws at
   102	the easy preset; all four records have `solved="error"`, HTTP 403, and the key
   103	limit message. The harness therefore produced no `harden_verdict`, correctly.
   104	
   105	| Arm | Solved / attempts | Conclusion |
   106	|---|---:|---|
   107	| bare | 0 / 0 scored | blocked by OpenRouter account limit |
   108	| structural hint | 0 / 0 scored | run blocked by account limit; no inference |
   109	| placebo hint | 0 / 0 scored | run blocked by account limit; no inference |
   110	
   111	The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is undefined until both scratch runs
   112	complete; the module currently records `0.0` only because both denominators are
   113	zero. No claim about hint usefulness should be drawn from it.
   114	
   115	## Use
   116	
   117	```python
   118	import importlib.util
   119	import json
   120	
   121	path = "results/1707.04310/gen_1707_04310.py"
   122	spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("gen_1707_04310", path)
   123	g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
   124	spec.loader.exec_module(g)
   125	
   126	inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   127	statement = g.render(inst)
   128	candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + json.dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
   129	assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
   130	```
   131	
   132	Because a dotted numeric directory is awkward in normal Python import syntax,
   133	loading by `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` is the portable alternative.
   134	From the repository root, emit examples with:
   135	
   136	```bash
   137	bash scripts/emit.sh 1707.04310 20
   138	```
   139	
   140	The module is standard-library-only; it does not need `gvlib`.
   141	
   142	## Caveats
   143	
   144	The measured random prior is uniform over all perfect matchings after enforcing
   145	the obvious shape and coverage constraints. It says nothing about a solver that
   146	recognizes complementation; that solver succeeds in linear time and is disclosed
   147	as the reference algorithm. Full lexicographic sorting or complement hashing
   148	also makes the family easy with tools. The four failing attacks cover label-
   149	frequency outliers, first-symbol greedy pairing, input adjacency, and 128 random
   150	restarts; they do not cover every possible language-model pattern heuristic.
   151	
   152	All paths have equal length and exactly half `a` symbols, and tuple order is
   153	uniformly shuffled, so those simple planting signatures are absent. The
   154	canonical key is exact under path reordering and global reverse-plus-label-swap;
   155	its SHA-256 digest retains only the usual theoretical collision caveat. Most
   156	importantly, the unavailable oracle evidence remains a release blocker rather
   157	than being presented as hardness.

